  "query_preview": "..\n\nSE MI resides under the dry slot of northern Great Lakes low \npressure to start the day limiting any precip to isolated pockets of \ndrizzle. This gradually begins to change through the latter half of \nthe morning mainly towards the Saginaw Valley/Thumb as southwesterly \nwinds draw in moisture off Lake Michigan supporting widely scattered \nlight showers. Chances expand over the southern portions of the CWA \nlate this afternoon as a lobe of vorticity rounds the trough over \nthe area providing a bit more lift to squeeze out scattered light \nshowers which then persist into early tonight. System's secondary \ncold front sweeps across the region early Friday morning offering a \nwindow for better coverage before ushering in a true late fall \nairmass to close the week out. \n\n850mb temps fall sub 0C behind the front as troughing sinks over the \ncentral Great Lakes capping highs slightly below average in the 40s \nto 50 Friday. The now closed low likewise sinks southeast over lower \nMI Friday generating a generally unsettled day with periodic showers \nand breezy northwesterly winds making it feel even cooler than the \nhighs would suggest. Greatest shower coverage/intensity likely \nfocused over the Thumb as the low center tracks nearby wrapping in \nadditional lake moisture. Rain chances gradually winds down over the \ncourse of Friday night as troughing shifts towards the eastern Great \nLakes.\n\nTemperatures remain around early November averages Saturday though \nwill feel cool given the fall thus far. Cloud cover slowly erodes \nthrough the day as surface high pressure/drier air slowly expands \nover the region. Ridging then expected to govern conditions through \nthe first half of the new week promoting another drier stretch and \nmoderation back above normal temperature-wise.",
